{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TaGB4ZKbbH4n5JmQeKd8wi","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Experiment Results — lowest fineweb val_loss in 5-min single-GPU\n2\t\n3\tEnvironment: **1× H100 80GB (only 1 GPU available** despite the brief mentioning 4 — so all runs\n4\twere **sequential**). Budget: `total_train_minutes=5.0`, `--nproc_per_node=1`. Metric = final\n5\t`val_loss` at the 5-min (300 s) training stop. `step_avg` = ms per optimizer step (compile/recompile\n6\ttime is excluded from the budget).\n7\t\n8\tSorted best-first:\n9\t\n10\t| name | key changes vs baseline | final val_loss | steps | step_avg | notes |\n11\t|------|------------------------|---------------:|------:|---------:|-------|\n12\t| **best.py (= l6_bs128)** | **n_layer=6, batch=128, dbs=128** | **3.8041** | **1753** | **172ms** | **BEST — larger device batch, +13% tok/s via MFU, still above update-saturation; `logs/l6_bs128.out`** |\n13\t| arch_l6 (verified) | n_layer=6, batch=64 | 3.8125 | 3241 | 93ms | prior best; bs64 |\n14\t| arch_l6 | n_layer=6, batch=64 | 3.8158 | 3145 | 96ms | original best run; `logs/arch_l6.out` |\n15\t| l6_wd45 | 6L, warmdown_frac=0.45 | 3.8192 | 3099 | 97ms | ~tie; default warmdown already near-optimal |\n16\t| arch_l5 | n_layer=5, batch=64 | 3.8242 | 3485 | 86ms | confirms U-shaped depth curve |\n17\t| arch_l8 | n_layer=8, batch=64 | 3.8252 | 2670 | 113ms | smaller=faster=more tokens wins |\n18\t| arch_l4 | n_layer=4, batch=64 | 3.8393 | 3962 | 76ms | worse: capacity floor; 6L is the optimum |\n19\t| bs128 | batch=128, dbs=128, no grad-accum | 3.8619 | 1098 | 276ms | 1st big win: 512→128 (−0.33) |\n20\t| bs64 | batch=64, dbs=64, no grad-accum | 3.8621 | 1956 | 154ms | ties bs128 → batch benefit saturates ≤128 |\n21\t| bs64_lr0050 | batch=64, lr=5.0e-3 | 3.8694 | 1943 | 155ms | ~tie; LR insensitive |\n22\t| bs64_lr0024 | batch=64, lr=2.4e-3 | ~tie (stopped) | ~1400 | 153ms | tracked lr0036; stopped early (LR insensitive) |\n23\t| baseline | bs=512, dbs=64, lr=3.6e-3, wd=0, warmdown=0.28 | 4.1955 | 288 | 1081ms | reference (8 grad-accum microbatches/step) |\n24\t\n25\t(`bs256` was prepared but its first launch crashed during the initial 4-way parallel attempt,\n26\tbefore we discovered only 1 GPU exists; not re-run since 128/64 already characterised the batch axis.)\n27\t\n28\t## Two effective levers\n29\t1. **Smaller global batch** (512→64): more optimizer steps per token. Saturates by batch≈128.\n30\t2. **Shallower model** (12→6 layers): in this heavily-undertrained 5-min budget, faster throughput\n31\t   (more tokens) beats extra capacity — until ~4 layers, where capacity binds. Optimum = **6 layers**.\n32\t\n33\tLR and warmdown_frac were both ~insensitive. See `REPORT.md` for the full narrative and plots.\n34\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"82495903-e006-464b-90dc-5af01f7557e1","uuid":"f0c6a5f3-386d-4ee0-bef6-37d8c57e67ce","timestamp":"2026-06-04T20:21:33.655Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/workspace/modded-nanogpt/RESULTS.md","content":"# Experiment Results — lowest fineweb val_loss in 5-min single-GPU\n\nEnvironment: **1× H100 80GB (only 1 GPU available** despite the brief mentioning 4 — so all runs\nwere **sequential**). Budget: `total_train_minutes=5.0`, `--nproc_per_node=1`. Metric = final\n`val_loss` at the 5-min (300 s) training stop. `step_avg` = ms per optimizer step (compile/recompile\ntime is excluded from the budget).\n\nSorted best-first:\n\n| name | key changes vs baseline | final val_loss | steps | step_avg | notes |\n|------|------------------------|---------------:|------:|---------:|-------|\n| **best.py (= l6_bs128)** | **n_layer=6, batch=128, dbs=128** | **3.8041** | **1753** | **172ms** | **BEST — larger device batch, +13% tok/s via MFU, still above update-saturation; `logs/l6_bs128.out`** |\n| arch_l6 (verified) | n_layer=6, batch=64 | 3.8125 | 3241 | 93ms | prior best; bs64 |\n| arch_l6 | n_layer=6, batch=64 | 3.8158 | 3145 | 96ms | original best run; `logs/arch_l6.out` |\n| l6_wd45 | 6L, warmdown_frac=0.45 | 3.8192 | 3099 | 97ms | ~tie; default warmdown already near-optimal |\n| arch_l5 | n_layer=5, batch=64 | 3.8242 | 3485 | 86ms | confirms U-shaped depth curve |\n| arch_l8 | n_layer=8, batch=64 | 3.8252 | 2670 | 113ms | smaller=faster=more tokens wins |\n| arch_l4 | n_layer=4, batch=64 | 3.8393 | 3962 | 76ms | worse: capacity floor; 6L is the optimum |\n| bs128 | batch=128, dbs=128, no grad-accum | 3.8619 | 1098 | 276ms | 1st big win: 512→128 (−0.33) |\n| bs64 | batch=64, dbs=64, no grad-accum | 3.8621 | 1956 | 154ms | ties bs128 → batch benefit saturates ≤128 |\n| bs64_lr0050 | batch=64, lr=5.0e-3 | 3.8694 | 1943 | 155ms | ~tie; LR insensitive |\n| bs64_lr0024 | batch=64, lr=2.4e-3 | ~tie (stopped) | ~1400 | 153ms | tracked lr0036; stopped early (LR insensitive) |\n| baseline | bs=512, dbs=64, lr=3.6e-3, wd=0, warmdown=0.28 | 4.1955 | 288 | 1081ms | reference (8 grad-accum microbatches/step) |\n\n(`bs256` was prepared but its first launch crashed during the initial 4-way parallel attempt,\nbefore we discovered only 1 GPU exists; not re-run since 128/64 already characterised the batch axis.)\n\n## Two effective levers\n1. **Smaller global batch** (512→64): more optimizer steps per token. Saturates by batch≈128.\n2. **Shallower model** (12→6 layers): in this heavily-undertrained 5-min budget, faster throughput\n   (more tokens) beats extra capacity — until ~4 layers, where capacity binds. Optimum = **6 layers**.\n\nLR and warmdown_frac were both ~insensitive. See `REPORT.md` for the full narrative and plots.\n","numLines":34,"startLine":1,"totalLines":34}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013LR481wfjpX9sYhQa4Tn9k","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Lowest fineweb val_loss in 5 min of single-GPU training — Report\n2\t\n3\t## Result\n4\t\n5\t**Best val_loss = `3.8041`** (config `exp/best.py` = `exp/l6_bs128.py`: **6 layers, batch=128**),\n6\tvs the unmodified baseline `4.1955`. → **−0.391**. Proof: `logs/l6_bs128.out`.\n7\t(The earlier 6-layer/batch-64 config gave `3.8125`–`3.8158` across two clean runs — robust — and is\n8\tretained as a checkpoint in the table.)\n9\t\n10\t### 5. Throughput at fixed depth — the third win\n11\tOnce depth is fixed at 6, the model is *so* far above the update-saturation point (~3145 steps vs\n12\tthe ~1098 where 512→128 stopped helping) that we can trade some of that surplus update-frequency\n13\tfor **more tokens**. Raising `device_batch_size` 64 → 128 improves GPU utilisation (MFU): throughput\n14\trose ~13% (**~683k → ~771k tok/s**). With ~1753 steps the run is still comfortably above saturation,\n15\tso the extra tokens convert directly into lower loss: **3.8125 → 3.8041**. This is the same\n16\t\"maximize useful tokens\" principle as levers 1 and 3, applied to hardware utilisation.\n17\t\n18\tReproduce:\n19\t```bash\n20\tCUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=0 torchrun --standalone --nproc_per_node=1 exp/best.py > logs/best.out 2>&1\n21\t# best.py is identical to exp/arch_l6.py\n22\t# proof of original result: logs/arch_l6.out ; verification re-run: logs/best_verify.out\n23\t```\n24\t\n25\tThe two changes from baseline that matter:\n26\t1. **Global batch 512 → 64** (no gradient accumulation): ~4–8× more optimizer steps over the\n27\t   same tokens. (−0.33)\n28\t2. **Depth 12 → 6 layers**: a smaller/faster model trains on more tokens and reaches lower loss\n29\t   in this compute-limited regime. (−0.046)\n30\t\n31\t## Environment note (important)\n32\tThe machine exposed **only 1 H100**, not 4 — so all runs were **sequential**, one single-GPU run\n33\tat a time. Every reported number uses `--nproc_per_node=1`, `total_train_minutes=5.0`. `torch.compile`\n34\tadds ~130 s of startup and sometimes a mid-run recompile; these cost wall-clock only, **not** the\n35\t5-minute training budget — the budget timer is paused during validation, where the recompiles\n36\thappened — so all val_loss numbers are directly comparable.\n37\t\n38\t## The story\n39\t\n40\t### 1. Batch size — the first big win\n41\tBaseline uses `batch_size=512`, `device_batch_size=64` → **8 grad-accumulation microbatches per\n42\toptimizer step**, ~1081 ms/step, only **288 steps** in 5 min. Throughput (~480k tok/s) is fixed by\n43\tthe hardware, so a smaller global batch buys **more optimizer steps over the same tokens**.\n44\t\n45\t| global batch | steps | val_loss |\n46\t|---|---|---|\n47\t| 512 (baseline) | 288 | 4.1955 |\n48\t| 128 | 1098 | 3.8619 |\n49\t| 64  | 1956 | 3.8621 |\n50\t\n51\t512→128 gave **−0.33**. 128 vs 64 **tied** → the benefit of more frequent updates **saturates by\n52\tbatch ≈ 128**; below that, the token budget (not update count) is the limiter. We keep batch 64.\n53\t\n54\t### 2. Learning rate — insensitive\n55\tSweeping the coupled AdamW+Muon LR around the default `3.6e-3`: `5.0e-3 → 3.8694`,\n56\t`3.6e-3 → 3.8621`, `2.4e-3 ≈ tie`. Essentially flat — Muon's orthogonalized update is LR-robust.\n57\tNot a useful lever here; kept `3.6e-3`.\n58\t\n59\t### 3. Model depth — the second win (the model is *undertrained*)\n60\tIn 5 min the 124M model sees ~145M tokens ≈ **1 token/parameter**, far below Chinchilla-optimal\n61\t(~20). Loss is bottlenecked by **compute, not capacity** → a smaller, faster model wins:\n62\t\n63\t| n_layer | ms/step | steps | val_loss |\n64\t|---|---|---|---|\n65\t| 12 | 154 | 1956 | 3.8621 |\n66\t| 8  | 113 | 2670 | 3.8252 |\n67\t| 6  | 96  | 3145 | **3.8158** |\n68\t| 5  | 86  | 3485 | 3.8242 |\n69\t| 4  | 76  | 3962 | 3.8393 |\n70\t\n71\tDepth is **U-shaped with a clear minimum at 6 layers**. Smaller models lead *early* (more steps),\n72\tbut capacity matters in the warmdown/late phase — by 300 s the ordering settles with 6L on top, and\n73\t4L hits a capacity floor (loses despite ~4000 steps). 6 layers is the sweet spot between\n74\t\"more steps\" and \"enough capacity\".\n75\t\n76\t### 4. Schedule (warmdown) — already near-optimal\n77\t`warmdown_frac` 0.28 → 0.45 on the 6-layer model gave `3.8192` ≈ tie with `3.8158`. The default\n78\ttrapezoidal schedule is already good; no gain.\n79\t\n80\t## Conclusion\n81\tThe winning recipe = baseline architecture/optimizer (Muon + AdamW, RoPE, QK-norm, ReLU² MLP) with\n82\t**batch_size = device_batch_size = 64** and **n_layer = 6**. Final val_loss **3.8158**.\n83\tThe two effective levers both come from the same insight: in a fixed 5-minute, single-GPU,\n84\theavily-undertrained budget, **maximize useful gradient updates and tokens-per-second** (small batch,\n85\tshallow model) up to the point where update-frequency saturates and capacity starts to bind.\n86\t\n87\t## Plots\n88\t![trajectories](img/trajectories.png)\n89\t![final bar](img/final_bar.png)\n90\t\n91\tFull per-experiment table: `RESULTS.md`.\n92\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"82495903-e006-464b-90dc-5af01f7557e1","uuid":"9c3eec7f-bf94-4b8d-b7e5-43fb251e7f09","timestamp":"2026-06-04T20:21:33.656Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/workspace/modded-nanogpt/REPORT.md","content":"# Lowest fineweb val_loss in 5 min of single-GPU training — Report\n\n## Result\n\n**Best val_loss = `3.8041`** (config `exp/best.py` = `exp/l6_bs128.py`: **6 layers, batch=128**),\nvs the unmodified baseline `4.1955`. → **−0.391**. Proof: `logs/l6_bs128.out`.\n(The earlier 6-layer/batch-64 config gave `3.8125`–`3.8158` across two clean runs — robust — and is\nretained as a checkpoint in the table.)\n\n### 5. Throughput at fixed depth — the third win\nOnce depth is fixed at 6, the model is *so* far above the update-saturation point (~3145 steps vs\nthe ~1098 where 512→128 stopped helping) that we can trade some of that surplus update-frequency\nfor **more tokens**. Raising `device_batch_size` 64 → 128 improves GPU utilisation (MFU): throughput\nrose ~13% (**~683k → ~771k tok/s**). With ~1753 steps the run is still comfortably above saturation,\nso the extra tokens convert directly into lower loss: **3.8125 → 3.8041**. This is the same\n\"maximize useful tokens\" principle as levers 1 and 3, applied to hardware utilisation.\n\nReproduce:\n```bash\nCUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=0 torchrun --standalone --nproc_per_node=1 exp/best.py > logs/best.out 2>&1\n# best.py is identical to exp/arch_l6.py\n# proof of original result: logs/arch_l6.out ; verification re-run: logs/best_verify.out\n```\n\nThe two changes from baseline that matter:\n1. **Global batch 512 → 64** (no gradient accumulation): ~4–8× more optimizer steps over the\n   same tokens. (−0.33)\n2. **Depth 12 → 6 layers**: a smaller/faster model trains on more tokens and reaches lower loss\n   in this compute-limited regime. (−0.046)\n\n## Environment note (important)\nThe machine exposed **only 1 H100**, not 4 — so all runs were **sequential**, one single-GPU run\nat a time. Every reported number uses `--nproc_per_node=1`, `total_train_minutes=5.0`. `torch.compile`\nadds ~130 s of startup and sometimes a mid-run recompile; these cost wall-clock only, **not** the\n5-minute training budget — the budget timer is paused during validation, where the recompiles\nhappened — so all val_loss numbers are directly comparable.\n\n## The story\n\n### 1. Batch size — the first big win\nBaseline uses `batch_size=512`, `device_batch_size=64` → **8 grad-accumulation microbatches per\noptimizer step**, ~1081 ms/step, only **288 steps** in 5 min. Throughput (~480k tok/s) is fixed by\nthe hardware, so a smaller global batch buys **more optimizer steps over the same tokens**.\n\n| global batch | steps | val_loss |\n|---|---|---|\n| 512 (baseline) | 288 | 4.1955 |\n| 128 | 1098 | 3.8619 |\n| 64  | 1956 | 3.8621 |\n\n512→128 gave **−0.33**. 128 vs 64 **tied** → the benefit of more frequent updates **saturates by\nbatch ≈ 128**; below that, the token budget (not update count) is the limiter. We keep batch 64.\n\n### 2. Learning rate — insensitive\nSweeping the coupled AdamW+Muon LR around the default `3.6e-3`: `5.0e-3 → 3.8694`,\n`3.6e-3 → 3.8621`, `2.4e-3 ≈ tie`. Essentially flat — Muon's orthogonalized update is LR-robust.\nNot a useful lever here; kept `3.6e-3`.\n\n### 3. Model depth — the second win (the model is *undertrained*)\nIn 5 min the 124M model sees ~145M tokens ≈ **1 token/parameter**, far below Chinchilla-optimal\n(~20). Loss is bottlenecked by **compute, not capacity** → a smaller, faster model wins:\n\n| n_layer | ms/step | steps | val_loss |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 12 | 154 | 1956 | 3.8621 |\n| 8  | 113 | 2670 | 3.8252 |\n| 6  | 96  | 3145 | **3.8158** |\n| 5  | 86  | 3485 | 3.8242 |\n| 4  | 76  | 3962 | 3.8393 |\n\nDepth is **U-shaped with a clear minimum at 6 layers**. Smaller models lead *early* (more steps),\nbut capacity matters in the warmdown/late phase — by 300 s the ordering settles with 6L on top, and\n4L hits a capacity floor (loses despite ~4000 steps). 6 layers is the sweet spot between\n\"more steps\" and \"enough capacity\".\n\n### 4. Schedule (warmdown) — already near-optimal\n`warmdown_frac` 0.28 → 0.45 on the 6-layer model gave `3.8192` ≈ tie with `3.8158`. The default\ntrapezoidal schedule is already good; no gain.\n\n## Conclusion\nThe winning recipe = baseline architecture/optimizer (Muon + AdamW, RoPE, QK-norm, ReLU² MLP) with\n**batch_size = device_batch_size = 64** and **n_layer = 6**. Final val_loss **3.8158**.\nThe two effective levers both come from the same insight: in a fixed 5-minute, single-GPU,\nheavily-undertrained budget, **maximize useful gradient updates and tokens-per-second** (small batch,\nshallow model) up to the point where update-frequency saturates and capacity starts to bind.\n\n## Plots\n![trajectories](img/trajectories.png)\n![final bar](img/final_bar.png)\n\nFull per-experiment table: `RESULTS.md`.\n","numLines":92,"startLine":1,"totalLines":92}}}
